Ladybird keeps IndexedDB databases in the profile’s SQLite browsing
database, with a row for each database’s shape, a row for each record
with its index keys, and stable ids for object stores and indexes. The
other engines keep stable ids too, but store index records as rows of
their own, and read records from disk as transactions need them: Blink
in its SQLite backing store (content/browser/indexed_db/instance/
sqlite’s DatabaseConnection, with object_stores, indexes, records, and
index_references tables) or LevelDB, WebKit in SQLiteIDBBackingStore’s
ObjectStoreInfo, Records, and IndexRecords tables, and Gecko in a
SQLite database for each origin, with the object_store, object_data,
and index_data tables of dom/indexedDB/DBSchema.cpp. Ladybird instead
loads all of a storage key’s databases into memory when its first
client connects, and writes through on each commit, which keeps the
backend’s record and index code the same with or without persistence.
